  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe primary forecast concern this afternoon and evening is the\npotential for isolated to scattered, heavy rain-producing\nthunderstorms ahead of an approaching cold front. This front,\ncurrently located across southern Wisconsin, will move southward\ninto northern Illinois during peak heating this afternoon.\nDespite mostly cloudy skies, occasional breaks will support\nmodest surface heating and the gradual destabilization of the\nvery moist, prefrontal airmass. Accordingly, as capping\ncontinues to weaken this afternoon, isolated showers and storms\nare expected to develop. Given limited surface convergence \nalong the boundary, storm coverage should remain isolated to \nwidely scattered for the next few hours. However, coverage may \nincrease along the front late this afternoon and into the \nevening as forcing for ascent strengthens in response to a \nremnant MCV moving east out of southern Iowa. If this occurs, it\nwould likely impact portions of the Chicago metro area and \nnorthwestern Indiana between 4 p.m. and 9 p.m. While the threat\nof severe weather remains low due to poor mid-level lapse \nrates, some locally gusty surface winds up to 50 mph could \noccur, particularly where storms cluster. Of greater concern are\nthe torrential downpours likely to accompany these storms, \nwhich could result in localized flooding, especially if multiple\nstorms track over the same areas of the Chicago metro.\n\nWe will see an end to our chances for showers and thunderstorms\nwith the passage of this front this evening. Surface high\npressure will settle over the Mid-Mississippi Valley on Monday.\nLower humidity and slightly cooler temperatures (highs around\n80) will provide a brief reprieve from our active weather\npattern of late, before another round of convection arrives late\nTuesday afternoon and night. This next system, driven by a cold\nfront and shortwave digging into the Great Lakes, brings a\nrenewed threat of strong storms and heavy rain Tuesday night.\nQuieter, cooler weather (temperatures in the 70s) returns for\nthe second half of the work week.\n\nKJB",
